1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,684
Every minute, your heart pumps about five litres

2
00:00:02,684 --> 00:00:05,201
of blood: roughly all the blood in your body.

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:09,675
Let's see how this fist-sized muscle does it, one step at a time.

4
00:00:10,866 --> 00:00:15,511
We'll look at the heart's two pumps, its chambers and valves, the path that blood

5
00:00:15,511 --> 00:00:20,212
takes, what happens in a single beat, and the electrical spark that starts it all.

6
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:24,163
The heart is really two pumps, side by side.

7
00:00:24,533 --> 00:00:27,442
The right side receives oxygen-poor blood returning

8
00:00:27,442 --> 00:00:29,781
from the body, and pumps it to the lungs.

9
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:34,565
In the lungs, blood drops off carbon dioxide and picks up fresh oxygen.

10
00:00:34,966 --> 00:00:37,869
The left side receives this oxygen-rich blood, and

11
00:00:37,869 --> 00:00:40,829
pumps it out to every organ and muscle in the body.

12
00:00:41,233 --> 00:00:44,008
There, cells use up the oxygen, and the blood

13
00:00:44,008 --> 00:00:47,154
returns to the right side, to start the loop again.

14
00:00:47,566 --> 00:00:50,526
This is called the double circulation: a short loop through

15
00:00:50,526 --> 00:00:53,034
the lungs, and a long loop through the whole body.

16
00:00:53,466 --> 00:00:55,544
By the way, blood is always red.

17
00:00:55,724 --> 00:01:00,008
Diagrams colour oxygen-poor blood blue, simply to tell the two apart.

18
00:01:01,230 --> 00:01:03,633
Inside, the heart has four chambers.

19
00:01:04,830 --> 00:01:09,439
The two upper chambers, the atria, are receiving rooms, where blood arrives.

20
00:01:09,830 --> 00:01:14,067
The two lower chambers, the ventricles, are the pumps that push blood out.

21
00:01:14,463 --> 00:01:17,666
A muscular wall called the septum divides the two

22
00:01:17,666 --> 00:01:21,197
sides, so oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor blood never mix.

23
00:01:21,596 --> 00:01:24,828
Notice that in diagrams like this, the heart's right side

24
00:01:24,828 --> 00:01:27,947
appears on your left, as if you were facing the person.

25
00:01:29,130 --> 00:01:31,928
Four one-way valves keep blood moving forward.

26
00:01:32,108 --> 00:01:36,125
The tricuspid and mitral valves sit between the atria and the ventricles.

27
00:01:36,496 --> 00:01:39,445
The pulmonary and aortic valves guard the exits.

28
00:01:40,430 --> 00:01:44,549
And the left ventricle has the thickest wall of all, because it pushes blood

29
00:01:44,549 --> 00:01:48,615
around the entire body, at about five times the pressure of the right side.

30
00:01:49,797 --> 00:01:51,991
Let's follow a single drop of blood.

31
00:01:52,363 --> 00:01:56,343
Oxygen-poor blood returns from the body through two large veins,

32
00:01:56,343 --> 00:02:00,386
the superior and inferior vena cava, and enters the right atrium.

33
00:02:00,763 --> 00:02:04,142
It passes through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle.

34
00:02:04,530 --> 00:02:08,099
The right ventricle contracts, pushing it through the pulmonary

35
00:02:08,099 --> 00:02:11,159
valve, into the pulmonary artery, and on to the lungs.

36
00:02:11,530 --> 00:02:17,242
In the lungs, it drops off carbon dioxide and picks up oxygen, turning oxygen-rich.

37
00:02:17,630 --> 00:02:20,834
It returns through the pulmonary veins into the left atrium.

38
00:02:21,230 --> 00:02:23,912
Then through the mitral valve, into the left ventricle.

39
00:02:24,297 --> 00:02:28,650
Finally, the powerful left ventricle squeezes it through the aortic valve into

40
00:02:28,650 --> 00:02:32,725
the aorta, the body's largest artery, which carries it to the whole body.

41
00:02:33,097 --> 00:02:36,475
Both sides do this at the same time, with every single beat.

42
00:02:39,197 --> 00:02:43,457
Each heartbeat is a precisely timed sequence, called the cardiac cycle.

43
00:02:43,830 --> 00:02:48,026
First, while the heart relaxes, a phase called diastole, blood flows into

44
00:02:48,026 --> 00:02:52,108
the atria, and straight on through the open valves into the ventricles.

45
00:02:52,497 --> 00:02:57,291
Next, the atria contract, topping up the ventricles with a final push.

46
00:02:57,663 --> 00:02:59,428
Then the ventricles contract.

47
00:02:59,608 --> 00:03:00,850
This is systole.

48
00:03:01,030 --> 00:03:03,952
The rising pressure snaps the tricuspid and mitral

49
00:03:03,952 --> 00:03:06,406
valves shut, making the first heart sound.

50
00:03:07,897 --> 00:03:10,824
The pulmonary and aortic valves are forced open, and

51
00:03:10,824 --> 00:03:13,469
blood rushes out, to the lungs and to the body.

52
00:03:13,863 --> 00:03:17,713
As the ventricles relax, the pressure drops, and the pulmonary

53
00:03:17,713 --> 00:03:21,003
and aortic valves snap shut, making the second sound.

54
00:03:26,097 --> 00:03:30,114
At rest, this happens around sixty to one hundred times every minute.

55
00:03:31,297 --> 00:03:33,386
What keeps this rhythm so steady?

56
00:03:33,566 --> 00:03:36,120
The heart has its own built-in electrical system.

57
00:03:36,497 --> 00:03:39,181
Each beat starts in the sinoatrial node, the heart's

58
00:03:39,181 --> 00:03:41,814
natural pacemaker, in the wall of the right atrium.

59
00:03:42,197 --> 00:03:46,202
Its signal spreads across both atria, making them contract.

60
00:03:46,597 --> 00:03:50,546
The signal then reaches the atrioventricular node, which holds it back

61
00:03:50,546 --> 00:03:54,271
for about a tenth of a second, giving the ventricles time to fill.

62
00:03:54,663 --> 00:03:58,034
Next, it races down the bundle of His through the septum, and

63
00:03:58,034 --> 00:04:01,570
spreads out along the Purkinje fibres, so the ventricles squeeze

64
00:04:01,570 --> 00:04:04,775
from the bottom up, like squeezing toothpaste from a tube.

65
00:04:05,163 --> 00:04:08,542
An ECG records this electrical activity through the skin.

66
00:04:08,930 --> 00:04:11,136
The P wave is the atria firing.

67
00:04:11,530 --> 00:04:14,537
The QRS complex is the ventricles firing.

68
00:04:14,930 --> 00:04:18,691
And the T wave is the ventricles recharging, ready for the next beat.

69
00:04:19,863 --> 00:04:21,523
Let's put some numbers on it.

70
00:04:21,703 --> 00:04:24,512
A resting heart beats about seventy times a minute.

71
00:04:24,896 --> 00:04:28,205
Each beat pumps about seventy millilitres from each ventricle.

72
00:04:28,596 --> 00:04:31,231
That adds up to about five litres every minute.

73
00:04:31,629 --> 00:04:34,596
And around one hundred thousand beats, moving some

74
00:04:34,596 --> 00:04:37,504
seven thousand litres of blood, every single day.

75
00:04:38,596 --> 00:04:39,409
To recap.

76
00:04:39,796 --> 00:04:42,199
The right side pumps blood to the lungs.

77
00:04:42,396 --> 00:04:44,439
The left side pumps it to the body.

78
00:04:44,629 --> 00:04:46,870
Four valves keep it flowing one way.

79
00:04:47,063 --> 00:04:49,744
And an electrical signal times every beat.

80
00:04:51,029 --> 00:04:54,512
This video was made entirely with free and open-source software.

81
00:04:54,692 --> 00:04:56,109
Thanks for watching.

